    scope: SUMER,
    topics: [{ goal: "worship" }, { occasion: "day.patron-offering" }, { scene: "offer" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Standing in prayer: the statues of Tell Asmar",
    text: "In Sumer every city belonged to a god, whose house, the temple, owned land, herds and workers. Ordinary people seldom entered the inner shrine. Instead, those who could afford it placed a statue of themselves in the temple to stand before the god forever: the hoard found at Tell Asmar holds men and women with clasped hands and huge inlaid eyes, fixed in prayer. Others offered bread, beer, oil or a lamb, which fed the temple staff after the god had 'eaten'. At home, a family also kept a personal god, who spoke for them to the great gods, and whose anger explained misfortune.",
