    scope: OLD_BAB,
    topics: [{ occasion: "day.borrow" }, { occasion: "day.debt" }, { aim: "restore-household" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Barley loans and the king's debt release",
    text: "Old Babylonian farmers borrowed barley before harvest and silver for taxes or emergencies, on tablets naming lender, amount, witnesses and due date. The customary rate was a third for barley and a fifth for silver. When harvests failed, debts piled up, and a debtor might pledge a wife, son or daughter to work in the creditor's house; Hammurabi's laws limited such bondage to three years. Kings also proclaimed a mīšarum, 'justice', usually on taking the throne, cancelling consumer debts and freeing debt-slaves. The edict of Ammi-saduqa spells it out, and says any creditor who hid a tablet to escape the decree would be put to death.",
    wiki: ["Ammi-Saduqa", "Code of Hammurabi"],
