    title: "Donkey caravans from Assur to Kanesh",
    text: "Around 1900 BCE, families of Assur ran a trade with central Anatolia: tin from the east and woollen cloth, much of it woven by the merchants' own wives and daughters at home, went by black donkey over some 1,000 km to Kanesh, near Kayseri. Each donkey carried about 65 kg of tin and ten textiles; the journey took six weeks. At Kanesh, merchants lived in their own quarter, the kārum, and sold for silver and gold, which went back to Assur. Over 23,000 tablets survive from their houses, including letters between husbands and wives: wives complain that money never arrives, and husbands that the cloth is too thin.",
