    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The great grain silos of Hattusa",
    text: "Near the postern wall of Hattusa, the Hittite capital, excavators found a sunken grain store of 32 chambers, sealed under earth, that could hold several thousand tonnes of grain, enough, by estimates, to feed tens of thousands for a year. Some grain was still inside, charred when the building burned. Grain was sealed under clay and earth to keep out air and pests, and could last for years. Villages kept their own stores in pits and jars, and the laws punish theft from a granary heavily. For a land with frequent drought, a full store was the difference between a hard year and famine.",
