    title: "Sennacherib's canals and the Jerwan aqueduct",
    text: "Around 700 BCE Sennacherib built a network of canals some 150 km long to bring mountain water to Nineveh and its orchards. At Jerwan the water crossed a valley on a stone aqueduct of about two million blocks, with pointed arches, the oldest aqueduct of its size known. His inscriptions boast that the new gardens grew cotton, 'trees bearing wool', and plants from every land. For ordinary townspeople, the result was water in canals through the city; for the villages upstream, it meant their streams were diverted. The canal heads carry reliefs of the king and gods, to claim the water as a royal gift.",
