    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The logbook of Merer and the pyramid gangs",
    text: "The Great Pyramid was not built by slaves. Workers were organised in named gangs of about 200, divided into phyles and smaller divisions, and fed from bakeries and breweries in a town south of the Giza plateau, Heit el-Ghurab, where bones show they ate a lot of cattle, sheep and goat. Some were full-time craftsmen; many were farmers serving a rotation. In 2013 papyri found at Wadi al-Jarf gave the logbook of Merer, an inspector who spent months ferrying limestone blocks by boat from the Tura quarries across the Nile to Giza, two or three trips every ten days, around 2560 BCE. Their graves near the pyramids hold their own offerings.",
