    id: "lore.bedouin.coffee",
    scope: { years: [1500, 1950], cultures: WA, bounds: [30, 15, 56, 36] },
    topics: [{ goal: "neighbour" }, { occasion: "day.friend" }, { scene: "warm" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Three cups at the coffee hearth",
    text: "Hospitality is the first law of the desert: a guest, even a stranger or enemy, can claim food, shelter and protection for three days without being asked his business. After coffee spread through Arabia from Yemen in the 16th century, it became the heart of the welcome. The host roasted the beans in a long-handled pan over the fire, ground them in a wooden or brass mortar whose ringing announced to the camp that guests had come, boiled them with cardamom, and poured small cups from a beaked pot. A guest took one, two or three, then shook the cup to say enough. Refusing coffee could signal a grievance to be settled.",
    wiki: ["Arabic coffee", "Bedouin"],
