    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Coffee terraces and the port of Mocha",
    text: "For two centuries, almost all the world's coffee came from the terraced mountains of Yemen. Sufis there had adopted the drink by the 15th century to keep awake for night prayers. Farmers grew the bushes on stone terraces in the western highlands, shaded and watered by springs and runoff, picked the red cherries by hand and dried them in the sun. Beans came down by camel to the markets of Bayt al-Faqih, where merchants from Cairo, India and later the Dutch, English and French bought them for the port of Mocha. Once Europeans planted coffee in Java and the Caribbean after 1700, Yemen's monopoly ended.",
