    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The great migration of the Bakhtiari",
    text: "Iran's pastoral tribes, the Bakhtiari and Qashqai above all, lived by moving flocks of sheep and goats between winter pastures in the lowlands of Khuzestan and Fars and summer pastures high in the Zagros, some 300 km apart. Each spring whole clans, perhaps tens of thousands of people, crossed snowbound passes and swam flocks and pack animals over raging rivers, a journey of weeks. Women wove the black tents, packed and unpacked camp daily, made butter and yoghurt and knotted rugs. Khans who led the tribes were powerful in Iranian politics, and Reza Shah tried in the 1930s to force the tribes to settle, with terrible losses of livestock.",
