    topics: [{ occasion: "day.quarrel" }, { occasion: "day.debt" }, { workplace: "civic" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Taking it to the kadı",
    text: "Every Ottoman town had a kadı, a judge trained in Islamic law, whose court was open to Muslims, Christians and Jews, men and women. His registers, the sicil, survive by the thousand and are full of ordinary life: debts acknowledged, loans recorded, quarrels over a wall or a water channel, a wife suing for unpaid maintenance, a slave claiming freedom, a guild complaining of an interloper. People came to court not only to fight but to register agreements, so they could be proved later. Many disputes ended in a sulh, a negotiated settlement witnessed by neighbours, which the court recorded. Christians and Jews often chose the kadı over their own courts.",
    wiki: ["Qadi", "Sharia", "Law of the Ottoman Empire"],
