    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Selamlık and haremlik: the Ottoman household",
    text: "A well-off Ottoman household was divided between the selamlık, where the men of the house received visitors, and the haremlik, the family quarters, ruled by the senior woman, often the husband's mother. Houses were of timber frame over a stone ground floor, with rooms that served many uses: bedding was rolled into cupboards by day, divans ran around the walls, meals were served on a tray on a low stand. Sons usually brought their brides into their father's house, so three generations lived together and parents aged at the heart of it; in villages, the flat-roofed single-storey house held the same arrangement. Respect for elders was shown by kissing the hand.",
    wiki: ["Selamlık", "Haremlik", "Ottoman architecture"],
