    scope: OTTOMAN,
    topics: [{ aim: "restore-household" }, { goal: "eat" }, { occasion: "day.borrow" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Soup from the imaret",
    text: "Great Ottoman mosque complexes included an imaret, a public kitchen funded by a pious endowment, a waqf. Twice a day it ladled out soup and bread to the staff of the complex, students, travellers and dervishes, and then to the poor of the neighbourhood, in set portions and order of precedence; on Fridays and holy days there was rice and meat, and zerde, saffron rice pudding. The Haseki Sultan imaret in Jerusalem, founded by Hürrem Sultan in the 1550s, fed hundreds daily. For the poor, the imaret, alms at mosque doors, and the neighbours' help in the quarter, the mahalle, were how a household survived a bad season without falling into debt.",
    wiki: ["Imaret", "Haseki Sultan Imaret", "Waqf"],
