    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Tarhana, bulgur and the winter store",
    text: "In Anatolian villages, late summer was for laying up the winter store, mostly women's work done together with neighbours. Wheat was boiled in great cauldrons, sun-dried on roofs and cracked in a mill or with a stone to make bulgur, which keeps for years. Tarhana was made from flour, yoghurt, tomatoes, peppers and onions fermented for days, dried in crumbs on cloths and later cooked as soup. Fruit was dried or boiled down into pekmez, grape molasses, and tomatoes and peppers dried or turned into paste. Cheese was packed in skins. A house with full bags of bulgur and tarhana hanging from its rafters was ready for the snows.",
    wiki: ["Tarhana", "Bulgur", "Grape syrup"],
