    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Silk-reeling girls of Mount Lebanon",
    text: "From the 1840s, French firms and then local owners built steam-powered silk-reeling factories across Mount Lebanon, taking the cocoons that peasant families raised on mulberry leaves and turning them into thread for the looms of Lyon. By 1900 there were nearly 200 factories, and most of the workers were young village women and girls, who stood at basins of hot water drawing out the filaments from dawn to dusk for low wages, many living in dormitories and sending pay home to fund dowries or a brother's emigration. Priests and families first objected to daughters working in mills. Mulberry covered the terraces, and silk paid for many village houses.",
