    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Long-staple cotton and the fellah",
    text: "In the 1820s Muhammad Ali's government began growing a long-staple cotton in the Delta, the Jumel variety, and made Egypt a cotton country. Cotton is a summer crop, and growing it meant replacing basin irrigation with deep canals that carried water all year, dug and cleared by the corvée: villagers forced to labour for weeks unpaid. The American Civil War sent prices soaring in the 1860s and fellahin took loans to plant more, then lost land when prices fell. Children picked cotton worms from the leaves by hand. By 1900 cotton was nine-tenths of Egypt's exports, and a family's fortunes rose and fell with the Alexandria price.",
