    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Oil at Masjed Soleyman and the Abadan refinery",
    text: "Oil was struck at Masjed Soleyman in the Bakhtiari country in 1908, and the Anglo-Persian Oil Company piped it to a refinery on the island of Abadan, which by the 1940s was among the largest in the world. The company recruited Iranian labourers, many of them tribesmen and villagers, alongside Indian clerks and craftsmen and British managers, and housed them in segregated quarters: bungalows and lawns for Europeans, crowded mud-brick shanties for Iranian workers. Pay was low, heat brutal. Workers struck in 1929 and again in 1946, and anger over who profited from the oil fed the nationalisation of 1951.",
