    title: "Losing the land in French Algeria",
    text: "After France invaded Algeria in 1830, the best land passed step by step to European settlers. Tribal and communal lands were declared state property, seized after revolts, above all after the 1871 rising in Kabylie, when whole tribes were fined and their land confiscated, and broken up by laws that made collectively owned land saleable. By the 1930s settlers held much of the fertile plain in wine and wheat estates. Algerian families pushed onto poorer hills became sharecroppers, khammès on a fifth of the crop, or seasonal labourers on colonial farms; many men emigrated to work in France and sent money home.",
