    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The Hejaz Railway to Medina",
    text: "Sultan Abdülhamid II built the Hejaz Railway from Damascus toward Mecca between 1900 and 1908, paid for largely by donations from Muslims worldwide, to carry pilgrims across a desert journey that had taken weeks by camel. Ottoman soldiers did much of the labour, laying track with German engineers. It reached Medina in 1908 and cut the pilgrimage to days. Bedouin tribes who had earned their living guiding, supplying and protecting the pilgrim caravans lost that income and attacked the line; in the First World War, Arab forces with T. E. Lawrence blew up its bridges and trains. Sections in Syria and Jordan kept running.",
