    evidence: "documented",
    title: "From kuttab to state school",
    text: "Through the 19th century, governments from Istanbul to Cairo and Tehran built new schools on European lines to train officers, officials and doctors: Ottoman rüşdiye middle schools, Muhammad Ali's schools of medicine and engineering, Iran's Dar al-Funun in 1851. Missionaries, American, French and Russian, opened schools too, including many of the first for girls, and in Beirut colleges that became universities. By 1900 an ambitious family could choose a kuttab, a state school, a mission school or a religious madrasa, and the choice shaped a child's career: clerk, teacher, lawyer or army officer. Literacy remained low in the countryside until after 1950.",
